/-!
# The diagonal of a smooth scheme is generated by a single external tensor product

Let `k` be a field and let `X` be a quasi-compact separated smooth scheme over `k`. The structure
sheaf `𝒪_Δ = Δ_*𝒪_X` of the diagonal, viewed in the derived category `D(𝒪_{X × X})`, lies in the
triangulated subcategory generated by a *single* external tensor product `ℰ ⊠ 𝒢` of two finite
locally free `𝒪_X`-modules. This is [Stacks, Tag 0FZ5], the "diagonal trick".

## The subcategory `⟨−⟩`

`⟨E⟩` is the notation of Derived Categories, Section 09SI: the smallest strictly full saturated
triangulated subcategory of `D` containing `E`. In this library that subcategory is
`CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.triangEnvelope` of the singleton property of `E`. That this is
the object of Section 09SI is the content of two results already in the library:
`CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.triangEnvelope_eq_iSup_and_isSaturated_and_le_iff`
([Stacks, Tag 0ATG]) proves it is strictly full, saturated, triangulated and smallest with those
properties, and
`CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.triangEnvelopeIter_eq_iSup_retractClosure_extensionProductIter`
([Stacks, Tag 0FX9]) identifies its stages with the Stacks project's
`⟨E⟩ₙ = ⋃_{m ≥ 1} smd(add(E[-m, m])^{⋆ n})`. The latter identification is what the proof below
actually consumes.

## The argument

`X` is regular by [Stacks, Tag 056S], hence has the resolution property by [Stacks, Tag 0F8A],
so [Stacks, Tag 0FZ2] provides a resolution

`… → ℰ₁ ⊠ 𝒢₁ → ℰ₀ ⊠ 𝒢₀ → 𝒪_Δ → 0`

by external tensor products of finite locally free modules. Brutally truncating it after `N`
steps gives a cochain complex `L` in degrees `[-N, 0]` whose terms are the `ℰᵢ ⊠ 𝒢ᵢ`, whose
zeroth cohomology is `𝒪_Δ` and whose cohomology vanishes in the intervening degrees. `X × X` is
smooth over `k`, hence regular of some finite dimension `d`; taking `N ≥ max d 1` makes the
cohomology gap of `L` at least `d` wide, so [Stacks, Tag 0FZ4] splits `L` in `D(𝒪_{X × X})` and
exhibits `𝒪_Δ` as a direct summand of it. Meanwhile [Stacks, Tag 0FX7] part (3) puts `L` itself
in `smd(add((ℰ ⊠ 𝒢)[-N, 0])^{⋆ (N + 1)})` for `ℰ = ⊕ᵢ ℰᵢ` and `𝒢 = ⊕ᵢ 𝒢ᵢ`, because each term
`ℰᵢ ⊠ 𝒢ᵢ` is a direct summand of `ℰ ⊠ 𝒢`. That subcategory is stable under retracts by
[Stacks, Tag 0FX4] and is contained in `⟨ℰ ⊠ 𝒢⟩` by [Stacks, Tag 0FX9], so `𝒪_Δ ∈ ⟨ℰ ⊠ 𝒢⟩`.

## Main definitions

The four hypotheses carried by the main theorem. Each is a statement about arbitrary data of its
own, mentioning no scheme, field or complex of the theorem it serves.

* `AlgebraicGeometry.HomVanishingAcrossCohomologyGap` is [Stacks, Tag 0FZ3] part (2) for every
  Noetherian scheme. It is the hypothesis that `AlgebraicGeometry.exists_iso_biprod_truncations`
  ([Stacks, Tag 0FZ4]) already carries, quantified over the scheme.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.FiniteDimensionalOfSmoothOverField`: a quasi-compact scheme smooth over a
  field has finite Krull dimension.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.BrutalTruncationOfAcyclicResolution`: the brutal truncation of an acyclic
  `ℕ`-indexed chain complex of modules, with its terms, its zeroth cohomology and the vanishing
  of its cohomology in the intervening degrees.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ExternalTensorSumRetract`: each `ℰᵢ ⊠ 𝒢ᵢ` of a finite family is a direct
  summand of a single external tensor product of two finite locally free modules.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.exists_externalTensor_triangEnvelope_diagonal`: **[Stacks, Tag 0FZ5]**.

## Implementation notes

`𝒪_Δ` is spelled `(Scheme.Modules.pushforward (pullback.diagonal f)).obj (SheafOfModules.unit _)`
and `ℰ ⊠ 𝒢` is `AlgebraicGeometry.externalTensorProduct`, the spellings of [Stacks, Tag 0FZ2] in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/ResolutionsAndBounds/DiagonalResolution.lean`; both are moved into
`D(𝒪_{X × X})` by `DerivedCategory.singleFunctor _ 0`. The derived category is read at
`CategoryTheory.HasDerivedCategory.standard`, as [Stacks, Tag 0FZ4] is, so that the splitting can
be applied without transporting along an equivalence.

"Quasi-compact separated smooth over `k`" is `[CompactSpace X] [IsSeparated f] [Smooth f]` for
`f : X ⟶ Spec k`. Separatedness over `Spec k` gives separatedness over the terminal scheme, which
is the form [Stacks, Tag 0F8A] consumes, because `Spec k` is itself separated over the terminal
scheme.

The conclusion is an existence statement, as the source's is: the source asserts that finite
locally free `ℰ` and `𝒢` exist, and no particular pair is singled out.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0FZ5](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FZ5)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0FZ2](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FZ2)
